; git/pr-review — answering a pull request as a bounded conversation.
;
; A review is not one question. It is: read what was proposed, read what people
; said about it, decide which remarks want an answer and which want a change,
; agree that plan with a human, do the work, and agree the result with the same
; human. Two of those steps are decisions no model may make for itself, and both
; are written here as host ports rather than as judge prompts — the reviewer is
; the one who says whether the plan is right, and the one who says whether the
; change is done.
;
; The two agreement loops are the shape of the module. `pr-settled` runs a piece
; of work, shows it to the host, and takes an exact 0 or 1 back. On 1 the answer
; stands. On 0 the host's own words become the revision instruction and the loop
; goes round again. There is no iteration count and no model-side "are we done
; yet": termination is the reviewer clicking accept, and the runtime's budgets
; are the backstop if nobody ever does.
;
; The plan is executed by writing a Rebis program and handing it back to the
; host to run as a nested run — see `pr-program`. That is the recursion this
; module is built around: a review that finds five threads writes a program with
; five stages, each of which may itself open a run. The host is responsible for
; running it and reporting back; the program is responsible for being explicit
; enough that running it is mechanical.
;
; Fetching is likewise the host's job. `pr-fetched` takes the reference through
; the port and asks for the pull request to be brought in with whatever tool the
; host has — `gh`, a browser, a checkout. Nothing here shells out; the module
; states what it needs and lets the runtime decide how.
;
; Cost note before the API: every macro states its own, but the shape of the
; whole is one fetch, four independent readings of the thread, one mediation,
; one plan, one program, and then two host-bounded loops. A review of a quiet
; pull request is a handful of calls. A contested one is however many rounds the
; reviewer asks for.
;
; The module routes itself. Judgement — reading a thread for what it is really
; asking, writing the plan, writing the program that carries it out — is bound
; to Opus 5. Clerical work — issuing the fetch directive, folding one objection
; back into a plan, restating what is outstanding — is bound to Haiku. The
; binding is written on the DEFINITION, so every call to that macro is routed
; whatever site calls it, and a caller that wants something else can still
; override its own subtree with a nearer `/`. Callers who want the whole review
; on one model write `(pr-review)/their-model` and the outer binding is what an
; unbound stage inherits; the stages bound here keep their own.
(
  (# std/assurance)
  (# std/canon)
  (# std/committee)
  (# std/seam)
  (# std/shape)

  ; (pr-fetched follow) — take a pull request reference from the host and bring
  ; the pull request in before anything reads it.
  ;
  ;   follow   a one-argument caller macro receiving the fetched pull request.
  ;
  ; The reference arrives through the port, so it is whatever the host sends:
  ; a URL, an `owner/repo#number`, or a branch. The fetch itself is a directive
  ; to the executing agent rather than a command this module runs, because the
  ; module cannot know whether the host has `gh`, a browser, or a working copy.
  ; If the agent has no way to reach the pull request it must say so plainly
  ; rather than describe one it has not read — that refusal is more useful than
  ; a confident summary of nothing.
  ;
  ; Cost: one port (free), one fetch call, plus whatever `follow` costs.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (pr-fetched pr-plan)
  (~ pr-fetched (follow)
    '(/ claude:haiku
       (= seam (&)
       (,follow
         (-> seam
           ($ "Fetch this pull request in full: its title, description, "
              "the complete diff, and every review comment with its file, "
              "line, author, and thread position. Use whatever tool you have "
              "for reaching it. If you cannot reach it, say exactly that and "
              "stop — do not describe a pull request you have not read."))))))

  ; (pr-threads pr) — read the comments as threads, not as a list.
  ;
  ;   pr   the fetched pull request. Read four times, independently.
  ;
  ; Four readers look at the same thread and are reconciled by a mediator. The
  ; repetition is the point: reviewers disagree about what a remark is asking
  ; for, and averaging that disagreement away before the plan is written is how
  ; a review ends up answering a question nobody asked. Each reading is told to
  ; separate what wants an ANSWER from what wants a CHANGE, because those are
  ; different obligations and conflating them is the usual failure.
  ;
  ; Cost: four readings of `pr`, one mediation.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (pr-threads the-pull-request)
  (~ pr-threads (pr)
    (/ claude:opus5
       (std-reconciled
      (-> pr
        ($ "Group every review comment into threads. For each thread state: "
           "who raised it, what file and line it concerns, what it is asking "
           "for, and whether it wants an ANSWER, a CHANGE, or both. Quote the "
           "comment. Mark anything already resolved by a later commit."))
      (-> pr
        ($ "Read the review comments adversarially. Which remarks are asking "
           "for something the diff does not do? Which are questions the author "
           "has not answered? Which are objections dressed as questions? State "
           "each with its file, line, and the obligation it creates.")))))

  ; (pr-plan pr) — the analysis and the todo list, in one document.
  ;
  ;   pr   the fetched pull request.
  ;
  ; The plan is deliberately two lists rather than one. Comments needing an
  ; answer and changes needing to be made have different completion conditions:
  ; one is done when a reply is posted, the other when the diff changes. A
  ; single "todo" column hides that, and a review that hides it tends to reply
  ; to everything and change nothing.
  ;
  ; Cost: `pr-threads` (four readings and a mediation), then one plan call.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (pr-plan the-pull-request)
  (~ pr-plan (pr)
    (/ claude:opus5
       (std-with-evidence
      (-> (pr-threads pr)
        ($ "Write the review plan as two numbered lists and nothing else.\n\n"
           "ANSWERS — comments that need a reply. For each: the thread, the "
           "question as asked, and the answer to post.\n\n"
           "CHANGES — work the comments require. For each: the file, what to "
           "change, and how anyone will know it is done.\n\n"
           "Put anything you judge out of scope in a third list called "
           "DECLINED, with the reason. An empty list is a valid answer; say "
           "so explicitly rather than inventing entries to fill it.")))))

  ; (pr-settled work revise) — hold a result open until the host accepts it.
  ;
  ;   work     the working whose result is being agreed. Fires once per round.
  ;   revise   a one-argument caller macro receiving the host's own words as
  ;            the revision instruction.
  ;
  ; The decision is a port, so it is an exact 0 or 1 from outside the program.
  ; No judge prompt, no model call for the verdict, and nothing a persuasive
  ; answer can do about it. Anything other than 0 or 1 raises InvalidCondition
  ; and runs neither branch.
  ;
  ; On rejection the loop does NOT simply retry: the host's message is passed to
  ; `revise` as the instruction, so a second round is directed rather than a
  ; second guess. This is the only loop in the module and both agreement points
  ; use it.
  ;
  ; Cost: per round, one firing of `work` and one of `revise`; the decision is
  ; free. Rounds are bounded by the host, not by the program.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (pr-settled (pr-plan the-pull-request) pr-revised)
  (~ pr-settled (work revise)
    '(= seam (&)
       (% (-> ,work
            ($ "Show this to the reviewer and ask whether it is right. "
               "Answer with exactly 1 if they accept it, or exactly 0 with "
               "their objection if they do not."))
          seam
          (pr-settled (,revise seam) ,revise))))

  ; (pr-revised note) — fold a reviewer's objection back into the plan.
  ;
  ;   note   the reviewer's own words, delivered by the port.
  ;
  ; The revision is told to change only what was objected to. A plan that is
  ; rewritten wholesale between rounds cannot be agreed, because the reviewer is
  ; reading a different document each time.
  ;
  ; Cost: one call.
  (~ pr-revised (note)
    (/ claude:haiku
      (-> note
      ($ "Revise the review plan to take this objection into account. Change "
         "only what the objection touches; leave every other entry exactly as "
         "it was, including its numbering. Restate the complete plan."))))

  ; (pr-program plan) — turn an agreed plan into a Rebis program the host can
  ; run as a nested run.
  ;
  ;   plan   the agreed plan, in the two-list shape `pr-plan` produces.
  ;
  ; The output is source, not prose: one stage per entry, arrows where a later
  ; stage needs an earlier answer, squares where two readings should stay
  ; independent until a mediator sees them, and a port wherever the host must
  ; supply something the program cannot know. Every prompt in it is written to
  ; be executable without further interpretation.
  ;
  ; This is the module's recursion. The generated program is handed back to the
  ; host, which runs it as a run nested under this one; a stage of that program
  ; may open a further run, and so on. Nothing here executes it — writing a
  ; program and running a program are different acts, and keeping them apart is
  ; what makes the plan reviewable before it costs anything.
  ;
  ; Cost: one call, plus whatever the generated program costs when the host
  ; chooses to run it.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (pr-program the-agreed-plan)
  (~ pr-program (plan)
    (/ claude:opus5
       (std-final-only
      (-> plan
        ($ "Write a Rebis program that carries out this plan. Emit source and "
           "nothing else — no explanation, no fences.\n\n"
           "Use one stage per plan entry. Use `->` where a stage needs the "
           "previous answer. Use `([judge] a b)` where two readings should "
           "stay independent until a mediator sees them. Use `(= name (&) body)` "
           "wherever the host must supply something the program cannot know, "
           "such as a command's output or a file's contents.\n\n"
           "Every prompt must be executable as written by an agent with the "
           "repository checked out. Name the file and the change in the prompt "
           "itself rather than referring back to the plan.")))))

  ; (pr-done program) — hand the program to the host, then agree the result.
  ;
  ;   program   the generated program, as source.
  ;
  ; The host runs it as a nested run and delivers what came of it; that delivery
  ; is the subject of the second agreement loop. The reviewer is looking at the
  ; work now rather than at the plan, so the loop is the same shape but the
  ; question is different: not "is this the right plan" but "is this done".
  ;
  ; Cost: one port for the run, then the agreement loop.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (pr-done (pr-program the-agreed-plan))
  (~ pr-done (program)
    (pr-settled
      '(= seam (&)
         (-> ,program
           ($ "Run this program as a nested run and report what came of it: "
              "which changes were made, which comments were answered, and "
              "what is still outstanding. Deliver the run's result through "
              "the port.")))
      pr-outstanding))

  ; (pr-outstanding note) — the closing list, when the reviewer is not done.
  ;
  ;   note   the reviewer's objection to the executed work.
  ;
  ; Produces the same two lists the plan used — changes made and comments still
  ; needing answers — so the second loop reads against the first. A round that
  ; reports in a new format cannot be compared with the one before it.
  ;
  ; Cost: one call.
  (~ pr-outstanding (note)
    (/ claude:haiku
       (-> note
      ($ "List what is still outstanding after this objection, in the same two "
         "lists the plan used: CHANGES still to make, and ANSWERS still to "
         "post. Include what is already finished under a heading DONE so the "
         "reviewer can see the whole state, not only the remainder."))))

  ; (pr-review) — the whole review, from a reference to an agreed result.
  ;
  ; Fetch the pull request, read its threads, plan the answers and the changes,
  ; agree the plan with the reviewer, write a program that carries it out, run
  ; it as a nested run, and agree the result with the same reviewer.
  ;
  ; Both agreements are host ports. The program will not proceed past either one
  ; until a person says so, and the reviewer's own words direct each retry.
  ;
  ; Cost: one fetch, four thread readings and a mediation, one plan, one
  ; program, and two host-bounded loops. Everything after the first agreement is
  ; paid only once the reviewer has accepted the plan.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (pr-review)
  (~ pr-review ()
    (pr-fetched pr-reviewed))

  ; (pr-reviewed pr) — the review of one already-fetched pull request.
  ;
  ;   pr   the fetched pull request.
  ;
  ; Split from `pr-review` so a host that already has the pull request in hand
  ; can start here without going through the port a second time.
  ;
  ; Cost: as `pr-review`, less the fetch.
  ;
  ; Example:
  ;   (pr-reviewed the-pull-request)
  (~ pr-reviewed (pr)
    (pr-done (pr-program (pr-settled (pr-plan pr) pr-revised))))
)
